To achieve this, you can use an Arduino Uno or any compatible board with a built-in USB serial port and connect it to your computer. Then, upload the following code to the Arduino board:

```cpp
#include <Adafruit_NeoPixel.h>

#define PIN 2
#define NUMBER_OF_LEDS 60

Adafruit_NeoPixel strip(NUMBER_OF_LEDS, PIN);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  strip.begin();
}

void loop() {
  if (Serial.available()) {
    int buttonPress = Serial.read();
    if (buttonPress == '1') {
      lightUpNextLed();
    }
  }
}

void lightUpNextLed() {
  for (int i = 0; i < NUMBER_OF_LEDS; i++) {
    uint32_t color = strip.Color(i % 7, 255 - i % 7, 0);
    strip.setPixelColor(i, color);
    delay(10);
  }
  delay(1000);
  for (int i = NUMBER_OF_LEDS - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    strip.setPixelColor(i, 0, 0, 0);
    delay(10);
  }
}
```

This code uses the Adafruit_NeoPixel library to control the LED strip. The `lightUpNextLed()` function will light up the next LED in the sequence and fade out the previous ones using a rainbow color scheme. When a '1' is received from the serial port, the function will be called, and the next LED will be lit up.

To test this code, open the Arduino IDE, copy and paste the code into a new sketch, and upload it to your Arduino board. Then, connect the board to your computer via USB and open the Serial Monitor. You can send '1' characters to the monitor to light up the next LED in the sequence.

To achieve this, you can use the following code:

```cpp
#include <WiFi.h>
#include <ESP32Servo.h>
#include <Adafruit_NeoPixel.h>

const int buttonPin = 2;
const int ledPin = 4;
const int numLeds = 60;

Adafruit_NeoPixel strip(numLeds, ledPin);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(buttonPin, INPUT_PULLUP);
  strip.begin();
}

void loop() {
  if (digitalRead(buttonPin) == LOW) {
    for (int i = 0; i < numLeds; i++) {
      strip.setPixelColor(i, strip.Color(random(255), random(255), random(255)));
      delay(100);
    }
    delay(1000);
  }
}
```

This code uses an ESP32 microcontroller and an Adafruit NeoPixel library to control the LED strip. It also includes a pull-up resistor on the button pin to detect when the button is pressed. When the button is pressed, the code loops through all the LEDs and sets their colors to a random value within the range of 0-255 for red, green, and blue components. The delay of 100ms between each LED update creates a fading effect, and the delay of 1000ms after all the LEDs have been lit ensures that the next press will start the cycle again.

To achieve this, you'll need to use the ESP8266 Arduino library and the FastLED library for controlling the LED strip. Here's a simple sketch to get you started:

```cpp
#include <ESP8266WiFi.h>
#include <FastLED.h>

// Replace with your WiFi credentials
const char* ssid = "your_ssid";
const char* password = "your_password";

// LED strip settings
#define LED_PIN D4
#define LED_COUNT 60
#define BRIGHTNESS 255

CRGB leds[LED_COUNT];

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  WiFi.begin(ssid, password);
  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) {
    delay(1000);
    Serial.println("Connecting to WiFi...");
  }
  Serial.println("Connected to WiFi");
  FastLED.addLeds<NEOPIXEL, LED_PIN>(leds, LED_COUNT);
  FastLED.setBrightness(leds, BRIGHTNESS);
}

void loop() {
  if (Serial.available()) {
    int buttonValue = Serial.read();
    if (buttonValue == '1') {
      // Light up the next LED
      int currentIndex = 0;
      while (currentIndex < LED_COUNT - 1) {
        leds[currentIndex] = CRGB::Red;
        currentIndex++;
      }
      FastLED.show();
      delay(500);
    }
  }
}
```

This sketch will read data from the serial port and light up the next LED when a '1' is received. You can modify the code to fit your specific needs and add the rainbow fading effect.

