Here's a simple After Effects script to create a vertical video as described:

```javascript
function main() {
    var project = app.project;
    var comp = project.activeItem;

    // Set up variables
    var file1Path = "C:\\Users\\UserName\\Desktop\\video1.mp4";
    var file2Path = "C:\\Users\\UserName\\Desktop\\video2.mp4";
    var textFile = "C:\\Users\\UserName\\Desktop\\textfile.txt";

    // Create a new composition
    var newComp = project.createComposition(640, 1080);
    comp.selection.selectAllElements();
    comp.duplicateSelection();

    // Import the video files
    var file1 = new CompItem(file1Path);
    var file2 = new CompItem(file2Path);

    // Add the video files to the new composition
    newComp.layers.add(file1);
    newComp.layers.add(file2);

    // Resize the layers to fit the composition size
    file1.resizeToFit(newComp.width, newComp.height);
    file2.resizeToFit(newComp.width, newComp.height);

    // Add a subtitle layer
    var subtitleLayer = newComp.layers.addText("Subtitle");
    subtitleLayer.position = [0, newComp.height - 50];
    subtitleLayer.fontSize = 36;
    subtitleLayer.textColor = [0, 0, 0];

    // Load the text file
    var text = File.read(textFile);

    // Add the text to the subtitle layer
    subtitleLayer.text = text;

    // Save the composition
    newComp.saveAs("vertical_video.mov");
}

main();
```

Replace the file paths with the actual paths to your video files and text file. This script will create a new composition, import the video files, resize them to fit the composition size, add a subtitle layer, load the text file from the specified path, and save the final composition as a movie

To fix the CORS error in WordPress when using Next.js, you can add the following code to your `next.config.js` file:
```javascript
module.exports = {
  // ...
  async headers() {
    return [
      {
        source: '/api/:path*',
        headers: [
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', value: 'true' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin', value: 'https://your-wordpress-site.com' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Methods', value: 'GET,OPTIONS,PATCH,DELETE,POST,PUT' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers', value: 'X-CSRF-Token, X-Requested-With, Accept, Accept-Version, Content-Length, Content-MD5, Content-Type, Date, X-Api-Version' }
        ]
      }
    ];
  }
};
```
Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com` with the URL of your WordPress site. This will allow Next.js to make requests to your WordPress API with the specified headers.

To fix the CORS error in NextJS when using WordPress as a backend, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `cors` package in your NextJS project:
```bash
npm install cors
```
2. Create a new file named `cors.js` in your `pages` directory and add the following code:
```javascript
import { Cors } from 'cors';

const cors = new Cors({
  methods: ['GET', 'POST'],
  allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization'],
  origin: 'http://your-wordpress-site.com' // Replace with your WordPress site URL
});

export default cors.createServer();
```
3. In your `next.config.js` file, add the following configuration to use the `cors.js` file:
```javascript
module.exports = {
  // ...
  serverMiddleware: [cors.createServer()],
};
```
4. Restart your NextJS development server and test your API routes again. The CORS error should be resolved now.

