To configure your Chrome extension's boilerplate files to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a new file named `inject.js` in your extension's folder.
2. Inject the script into the webpage by adding the following code to `manifest.json`:
```json
{
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
      "js": ["inject.js"]
    }
  ]
}
```
This will inject the `inject.js` script into every webpage the user visits.
3. In `inject.js`, add the code to inject the JavaScript on the webpage. You can use the `document.createElement` method to create a script element and then append it to the `head` of the document. Here's an example:
```javascript
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function() {
  var script = document.createElement("script");
  script.src = chrome.runtime.getURL("your-script.js");
  document.head.appendChild(script);
});
```
Replace `"your-script.js"` with the path to your actual JavaScript file.
4. Save both files and refresh your browser to see the injected script in action.

Remember to test your extension thoroughly to ensure that it works as expected and does not cause any errors or conflicts with other extensions or websites.

To configure your Chrome extension to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage, you can use the `chrome.tabs` API to create a content script that will run in the context of the visited page. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Create a new file called `inject.js` in your Chrome extension folder (usually `chrome/extensions/your_extension_id/`). This file will contain the content script code.
2. Add the following code to `inject.js`:
```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
  const script = document.createElement('script');
  script.src = 'path/to/your/javascript/file.js';
  document.body.appendChild(script);
});
```
Replace `'path/to/your/javascript/file.js'` with the actual path to your JavaScript file that contains the code you want to inject into the visited page.
3. In your Chrome extension manifest file (`manifest.json`), add the following code to the `content_scripts` section:
```json
{
  "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
  "js": ["inject.js"]
}
```
This will tell Chrome to inject the `inject.js` file into all visited pages.
4. Save both files and reload your Chrome browser to test your extension. The JavaScript file should now be injected into the visited pages.

Note: Make sure to follow Chrome's extension guidelines and best practices when developing your extension.

