response:
Here's a simple React utility to remove widows:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [formattedText, setFormattedText] = useState(text);

  const handleChange = (event) => {
    const newText = event.target.value;
    const words = newText.split(' ');
    const lastLine = words.pop();

    if (lastLine && lastLine.length > 0) {
      words.push(lastLine);
    }

    setFormattedText(words.join(' '));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <textarea value={formattedText} onChange={handleChange} />
      <p>{formattedText}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

This component takes a `text` prop and renders a text area and a paragraph element. The text area allows the user to enter text, and the paragraph element displays the formatted text with no widows. The `handleChange` function splits the input text into an array of words, removes the last word if it's too long, and joins the words back together with a space.

Here's a simple React utility to remove widows. This utility will check if a text node has a width less than its parent container and adjust the font size accordingly.

```javascript
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ children }) => {
  const [fontSize, setFontSize] = useState(16);

  useEffect(() => {
    const adjustFontSize = () => {
      const textNode = document.querySelector('.text-node');
      const parentContainerWidth = textNode.offsetParent.offsetWidth;
      const textNodeWidth = textNode.offsetWidth;

      if (textNodeWidth < parentContainerWidth) {
        setFontSize((prevFontSize) => prevFontSize - 2);
      }
    };

    adjustFontSize();
  }, []);

  return (
    <div className="text-node">
      {children}
      <style jsx>{`
        .text-node {
          font-size: ${fontSize}px;
        }
      `}</style>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this utility, you can wrap your text content with the `RemoveWidows` component like this:

```javascript
import React from 'react';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <div>
      <RemoveWidows>
        Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Sed non risus. Suspendisse lectus tortor, dignissim sit amet, adipiscing nec, ultricies sed, dolor. Cras mattis consectetur purus, sed tincidunt magna aliquet non.
      </RemoveWidows>
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

Here's a simple React utility to remove widows:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [updatedText, setUpdatedText] = useState(text);

  const handleRemoveWidows = () => {
    const words = updatedText.split(' ');
    const lastWord = words[words.length - 1];

    if (lastWord !== '') {
      words.pop();
      setUpdatedText(words.join(' '));
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={handleRemoveWidows}>Remove Widows</button>
      <p>{updatedText}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this component, simply pass in the text you want to remove widows from as a prop:

```jsx
<RemoveWidows text="Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit." />
```

